---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import { cliHelp, repo } from "../../lib/repo";
import { stages } from "../../lib/sources";

const newsHelp = cliHelp("news");
const sourcesHelp = cliHelp("sources");
const eolHelp = cliHelp("eol");
const cveHelp = cliHelp("cve");
const stageText: Record<string, string> = {
  "heads-up": "A security release is announced ahead. When the same feed announces the release itself, the item becomes a release.",
  release: "The fix is out.",
  advisory: "The details of a vulnerability: affected and fixed versions, score, conditions.",
  alert: "A CERT alert, a public exploit, or exploitation in the wild.",
  news: "Security press and research: context, never enough on its own to call a project affected.",
};
---
<Docs title="The CLI" description="stackalarm news, cve, eol and sources: options, output formats, stages, limits, cache and exit codes.">
  <p class="lead-in">
    The CLI gathers the news; it does no project analysis. The skill runs it for you, but it works on its own too. The help below is printed by
    the CLI itself when this page is built.
  </p>

  <h2 id="news"><code>stackalarm news</code></h2>
  <Code label="stackalarm news --help" code={newsHelp} copy={false} />

  <h3 id="tags">Tags and keywords</h3>
  <p>
    A tag selects the sources of a technology (<code>php</code>, <code>gitlab</code>…) and the general sources (CERTs, CVE databases, exploit
    feeds, press) whose entries mention it. A keyword only searches the general sources: use it for what has no tag (<code>roadrunner</code>).
    An unknown tag is searched as a keyword, with a warning that lists the known tags. Each tag and keyword costs one search per CVE database.
  </p>

  <h3 id="packages">Packages and versions</h3>
  <p>
    With an ecosystem tag (<code>npm</code>, <code>composer</code>, <code>pip</code>…), <code>--package</code> narrows the GitHub Advisory
    Database to the packages of the project. Give the locked version (<code>next@15.1.0</code>) and only the advisories that hit that version
    are returned; on a typical Next.js project, that leaves out two advisories in three. Each advisory shows the affected packages, the
    vulnerable range and the first fixed version, the range holding your version first.
  </p>
  <p>
    With versions, <a href="https://osv.dev">OSV.dev</a> is also asked which known vulnerabilities hit them, in one request for all the
    packages and without a token. Unlike the rest, these items are not news: they are listed whatever their date, because a version locked
    today is still exposed to a flaw published last year. Packages without a version are not sent to OSV, and <code>--no-osv</code> (or the
    <code>STACKALARM_NO_OSV</code> variable) turns it off.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tag takes a version too: <code>--tag php@8.3.20</code>. The NVD records give the vulnerable ranges of a product; each is compared with
    the version and marked <code>affected</code> or <code>not-affected</code> (<code>[8.3.20 in use: AFFECTED]</code> in the text formats),
    and the records that hit the version come first under the cap. Give a full version, never a branch. The products known for each tag are
    listed under <code>cpe</code> in <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/sources/search.yaml`}><code>sources/search.yaml</code></a>; a tag too
    ambiguous to be searched by word (<code>composer</code>, <code>flask</code>, <code>npm</code>…) is searched in the NVD by product instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    With <code>--package</code>, the GitHub malware advisories of those packages are read too, as alerts: a version published with malicious
    code, the mark of a compromised maintainer account. They are not read without packages: npm alone gets about a thousand a month.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sbom">Container images and SBOMs</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--sbom</code> reads a CycloneDX or SPDX document and looks up every package it lists on OSV.dev, system packages of a container
    image included (Debian, Ubuntu, Alpine, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, Wolfi, Chainguard). See <a href="/docs/sbom/">Container images</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="exploitation">Exploitation and EPSS</h3>
  <p>
    A CVE listed in the CISA KEV catalog or the EUVD list of exploited flaws is marked exploited wherever it appears, whatever the date it
    was added: an old flaw can be exploited today. Exploited CVEs are kept first under the cap. Each CVE also gets its EPSS, the probability
    of exploitation in the next 30 days computed by FIRST, from GitHub or from the FIRST API.
  </p>
  <p>
    A CVE that has a <a href="https://github.com/projectdiscovery/nuclei-templates">Nuclei template</a> or a
    <a href="https://github.com/rapid7/metasploit-framework">Metasploit module</a> is marked as having a public exploit: anyone can run the
    attack. Those two catalogs give no item of their own; they only mark the CVEs of the other sources.
  </p>

  <h3 id="stages">Stages</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {stages.map((s) => <tr><td><span class="stage" data-stage={s}>{s}</span></td><td>{stageText[s]}</td></tr>)}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="formats">Output formats</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Format</th><th scope="col">For</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>text</code></td><td>Reading in a terminal. The default.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>summary</code></td><td>Agents: one line per item (date, stage, source, score, title, link, other databases listing the same CVE, affected packages with the first fixed version and whether the version in use is in the range, EPSS, the sources reporting it exploited, the catalogs holding a public exploit), then the failed and truncated sources and the items the profile ignores.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>json</code></td><td>Scripts: the items with their summaries, plus <code>failures</code>, <code>truncated</code>, <code>short</code> and <code>warnings</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>markdown</code></td><td>An issue body, used by the scheduled watch.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sarif</code></td><td>Code scanning dashboards (GitHub, GitLab): SARIF 2.1.0 with only the items a version in use proves, that is a vulnerable range holding the version given, or a component past its end of life. The rest is raw news that only a reader can judge, and stays out. Each result points at the profile.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="limits">Limits, failures and cache</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Each source shows at most 30 entries. For the CVE databases, the most severe come first, so the cap drops the minor ones. A CVE found in several databases is shown once and takes one place. The output lists the sources that had more.</li>
    <li>Paginated APIs (GitHub, EUVD) are read page by page, up to 10 pages per query. A query that matches more is reported as a <code>partial</code> failure, with what was read.</li>
    <li>A source that cannot be read is always listed: a silent watch tool is worse than a noisy one.</li>
    <li>A feed keeps only its latest entries: when its oldest one is newer than the start of the period, it is listed as <code>short</code>, since older news may be missing. A scheduled watch that runs every few hours is not affected.</li>
    <li>Responses are reused for an hour, from <code>stackalarm</code> in your user cache folder. <code>--no-cache</code> fetches everything again.</li>
    <li>Without <code>NVD_API_KEY</code>, the NVD allows one request every 6 seconds; with it, about one every 0.7 seconds.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="exit">Exit codes</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Done, and nothing matched <code>--fail-on</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>An error: a bad option, an unreadable profile or state file.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>New items with one of the <code>--fail-on</code> stages were found.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 id="watch-flags">For a scheduled watch</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--profile</code> reads the tags, keywords and packages from <code>.stackalarm.yaml</code>, compares the exact versions of its stack
    with the ranges of the CVE records, and leaves out the vulnerabilities it ignores (they are still counted and listed). <code>--state</code> keeps a file of the items
    already reported, so only new ones are shown; items are forgotten after 90 days, and the file is replaced atomically, so an interrupted run
    keeps the previous state. <code>--fail-on</code> turns new items into exit code 2. See <a href="/docs/watch/">Scheduled watch</a>.
  </p>
  <Code code="stackalarm news --profile .stackalarm.yaml --state .stackalarm-state --fail-on heads-up,alert,release --since 48h --format markdown" copy={false} />

  <h2 id="cve"><code>stackalarm cve</code></h2>
  <Code label="stackalarm cve --help" code={cveHelp} copy={false} />
  <p>
    One CVE, every database: the <a href="https://www.cve.org">CVE Program</a> record with the CISA Vulnrichment assessment (SSVC:
    exploitation, automatable, technical impact), the NVD, the GitHub Advisory Database, CISA KEV and the EUVD list of exploited flaws, the
    catalogs of public exploits, and EPSS. A database that cannot be read is listed, and the others still answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="eol"><code>stackalarm eol</code></h2>
  <Code label="stackalarm eol --help" code={eolHelp} copy={false} />
  <p>
    A runtime past its end of life gets no fix for the next flaw, and no advisory will say so: the news only covers what is still
    maintained. <code>eol</code> reads <a href="https://endoflife.date">endoflife.date</a> for each component and gives its cycle, the date
    its security fixes stop, and the latest release of the cycle. Only names and versions are sent, never the project.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sources"><code>stackalarm sources</code></h2>
  <Code label="stackalarm sources --help" code={sourcesHelp} copy={false} />
  <p>Lists every source with its id, stage and tags. The same list is on the <a href="/docs/sources/">Sources</a> page.</p>
</Docs>
